WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.699
Popular TV series can become victims of their
own success.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:08.000
Cast and crew make such compelling television
that some fans just can't wait to find out

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.920
what happens next on their favorite shows,
and will go to any length to get that information.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:15.799
That's why some productions have tried to
thwart these spoilers at great effort and

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.700
expense by actually scripting and filming
scenes they never even intend to use.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:23.520
Here's a look at shows that tried to throw
off the spoilers with fake footage.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.220
And of course, real spoilers ahead…

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:26.740
The Sopranos

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:32.180
The Sopranos had one of the most famous — and
maddening — final moments in TV history.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.379
As gangster Tony Soprano sits in a diner with
members of his family and Journey's "Don't

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:40.420
Stop Believing" plays on the jukebox, the
scene abruptly cuts to black, mid-scene.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.640
Many viewers assume that the sudden ending
reflects Tony Soprano's fate — that he was

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.360
killed by one of his many enemies.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:51.000
In the decade since The Sopranos wrapped up,
creator David Chase has been reluctant to

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.240
spell out exactly what became of Tony, but
in 2016, he told Deadline that he shot a different

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.420
ending because the production had experienced
problems with "people trying to invade and

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:01.160
get our scripts."

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.730
The scene was shot, but the details were so
inconsequential — and destined to be discarded

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.820
as a ruse — that Chase couldn't even remember
all the details.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:13.600
He tried to recall: "Tony goes back to the
Bada Bing strip club and has an argument with

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:14.720
Silvio or something.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.660
Well, it couldn't have been because he was
in the hospital.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.020
Well, anyway, it was a fake ending that we
shot just to throw people off."

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:22.160
Lost

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:28.130
John Locke, played by Terry O'Quinn met a
sad fate on Lost, horrifically strangled to

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.009
death by Michael Emerson’s Ben Linus.

00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:34.820
But the show played fast and loose with time
and space, so Locke was still part of the

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:38.680
series even after the revelation that the
body in the casket in the funeral parlor was

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:39.200
his.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:45.100
Or maybe not: After a major plot in which
Locke led a group looking to take down Jacob,

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.460
his body was shown rolling out of a cargo
box.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:52.900
Heroic Leader Locke was actually the shape-shifting
Man in Black, and the real Locke was dead.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:57.320
To throw everybody for a loop, or maybe to
keep their options open, the Lost crew shot

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:01.120
a couple of other scenes, in which the dead
man in the coffin isn't Locke, but Sawyer

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.500
and Desmond instead.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.100
"He was a man of faith.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:12.660
He was… a much better man than I will ever
be.”

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:14.260
Sex and the City

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.200
HBO's Sex and the City was an updated, urban
take on the romantic comedy, but a romantic

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.510
comedy nonetheless, and it ended like one:
Sarah Jessica Parker’s character, Carrie

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:28.370
Bradshaw wound up with her on-again, off-again
guy, Mr. Big, and returned to New York after

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:28.940
a spell in Paris.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:34.280
The day after the finale aired in 2004, Parker
told USA Today that three different endings

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.960
had been shot, with the one true wrap-up kept
a secret.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:41.220
The abandoned endings, which Parker called
"just silly," involved Carrie returning to

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.800
New York alone — or with artist boyfriend
Aleksandr Petrovsky.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.240
Imagine the fan uproar if that happened.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:48.720
Dallas

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:56.320
A massive hit during the 1979 to 1980 TV season,
the CBS primetime drama Dallas entranced millions

00:02:56.330 --> 00:03:01.010
with the story of wicked Texas oil tycoon
J.R. Ewing, played by Larry Hagman and his

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:02.040
family and associates.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:06.800
In the cliffhanger third season finale, as
J.R. got ready for bed one night…someone

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:08.220
shot him and left him for dead.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:09.320
[Gunshot]

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:19.650
It was a shocking TV moment, and over the
summer, seemingly all of America debated "Who

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:23.120
shot J.R.?" — as pretty much every character
on Dallas had a motive.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:28.470
So to keep fans guessing in case a cast or
crew member leaked the details, multiple resolutions

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:29.660
to the cliffhanger were shot.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:35.079
So who really shot J.R.? Turns out it was
his mistress and sister-in-law, Kristin Shepard

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.560
portrayed by Mary Crosby.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:37.800
The Simpsons

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:42.660
Fifteen years after "Who Shot J.R.?," the
sixth season of The Simpsons ended with a

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:47.870
similar cliffhanger episode, in which Springfield
Nuclear Power Plant boss C. Montgomery Burns

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:53.670
is shot by an unidentified assailant and collapses
onto a sundial, with his arms pointed to the

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:58.969
"W" and "S." Pretty much everybody in Springfield
was a suspect, but in the seventh-season premiere,

00:03:58.969 --> 00:04:01.160
infant Maggie Simpson was revealed as the
shooter.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.640
While there were clues that the baby did it,
Simpsons writer David Mirkin told The A.V.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.400
Club that he "purposely wanted to construct
it so that it made you think that Smithers

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:09.920
had done it."

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:14.640
As discussed on a Simpsons DVD commentary
track, scenes of many characters pulling the

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.100
trigger were created, including Moe, Barney,
and Santa's Little Helper.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:24.150
To fool leakers further, a completely separate
ending was produced in which Smithers actually

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:25.560
did shoot Mr. Burns.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.280
The Walking Dead

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:32.800
The sixth season of The Walking Dead built
up to the arrival of Negan, played by Jeffrey

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:33.320
Dean Morgan.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:38.440
In the original comics written by Robert Kirkman,
Negan almost immediately kills Glenn with

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:41.100
Lucille, his barbed-wire wrapped baseball
bat.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:46.300
Would the show follow suit, or would another
member of Rick's gang, ambushed by the Saviors,

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:47.320
meet their doom instead?

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.740
In the mid-season finale, Negan killed…somebody.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.380
The show cut to black before we could find
out.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:57.800
Viewers had to wait a while to learn that
Glenn and Abraham were indeed at the other

00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:58.720
end of Lucille.

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:03.780
Showrunner Scott Gimple wanted to keep that
eventual outcome secret, which was especially

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:08.719
difficult because The Walking Dead deals with
incredibly bold and intrusive spoiler-hunters

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:12.780
— in fact, camera-equipped drones have been
spotted flying over the set.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:18.920
In order to preserve the big reveal, the show
shot 11 different Lucille-related deaths scenes:

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:19.860
one for each character.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:21.600
Game of Thrones

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:26.620
While The Walking Dead crew has to deal with
intrusive drones, Game of Thrones has to deal

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:31.530
with paparazzi using long-range camera lenses
to snap pictures of the show in progress.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:36.680
In the lead up to the show's seventh season
in 2017, producers filmed fake scenes to fool

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:39.340
those tricky photographers and spoiler-hungry
fans.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:44.759
Kit Harington, who stars as Jon Snow, told
Jimmy Kimmel that the series shot three scenes

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.360
that weren't part of the show or intended
to be used at all.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:52.720
"We put together people in situations where
we knew the paparazzi was around, so they’d

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.360
take photos and that would get on the internet,
to fake scenes.”

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:00.420
For his part, Kimmel summed up what the process
must've been like for the cast and crew:

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:02.360
"What a pain in your ass that is.”

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:03.680
Thanks for watching!

